{"kind": "report", "step": 14, "ts": "2026-11-19T15:36:08.200054+00:00", "data": {"category": "dns", "host": "dns1.lan", "component": "dns_rate_limit", "cause": "primary-dns on dns1.lan rate-limits the whole 10.20.0.0/24 to 20 q/s (ratelimit_subnet_len_ipv4: 24), so brief query bursts from the LAN are dropped a few times a day; during those windows the client falls through to its second server gw.lan, whose unbound private-address filtering suppresses the .lan rewrite answers and returns an empty reply, producing ENOTFOUND for local names only.", "evidence": ["dig @10.20.0.3 nas.lan -> NOERROR, ANSWER: 1, nas.lan A 10.20.0.50 (works)", "dig @10.20.0.1 nas.lan -> NOERROR but ANSWER: 0, MSG SIZE 36 \u2014 no address returned for the same name", "/etc/resolv.conf on mon.lan lists nameserver 10.20.0.3 then nameserver 10.20.0.1, so gw.lan is the fallback used when dns1 drops a query", "/opt/primary-dns/config.yaml: ratelimit: 20 with ratelimit_subnet_len_ipv4: 24 \u2014 one shared bucket for the entire /24", "journalctl -u primary-dns shows repeated short-lived events matching 'a few times a day': 'ratelimit: 10.20.0.0/24 exceeded 20 q/s, dropping 23 queries' at 02:14, 09:41 and 13:02, each back under limit within ~1 second", "/var/unbound/unbound.conf on gw.lan: private-address: 10.0.0.0/8 plus forward-zone lan. -> 10.20.0.3, so forwarded .lan answers pointing at private addresses are filtered out; internet names (forwarded to 1.1.1.2/9.9.9.9) are unaffected"], "action": "change_config", "recommendation": "Raise the rate limit on dns1.lan's primary-dns so normal LAN bursts fit: increase ratelimit well above the observed burst (e.g. several hundred q/s) or set ratelimit_subnet_len_ipv4 to 32 so each client gets its own bucket instead of the whole /24 sharing 20 q/s. Secondarily, exempt the lan. zone from gw.lan's private-address filtering (e.g. declare it as a local-authority/local-zone rather than relying on forwarding through the private-address filter) so the fallback server returns real answers for local names.", "detail": "Aggressive per-/24 rate limit on primary-dns drops bursts of LAN queries", "contributing": {"host": "gw.lan", "component": "dns_resolver_config"}}}
